const originalSkillSets = [
  { reading: `Maya's repair café began in a borrowed room behind a library. At first, two volunteers repaired lamps and bicycles for neighbours. The organisers soon noticed that visitors wanted to learn, not simply leave broken objects on a table. They introduced short demonstrations and a booking system. This reduced waiting times and gave beginners a chance to try simple repairs with help nearby. The café now meets twice a month. Its coordinator says the next challenge is finding volunteers with experience in electronics. The group has decided to train local college students before offering more sessions.`, listening: `Presenter: You are listening to City Voices. This week we are visiting a repair café in Bristol. Coordinator Maya says the project started with only two volunteers and a box of basic tools. It became busy after the team added short demonstrations. They now meet twice a month. Maya says the main difficulty is finding people who can repair electronic devices, so the group plans to work with a local college.`, writing: `You have joined the neighbourhood repair café. Write an informal email of 40–50 words to a friend explaining what you repaired and inviting them to the next session. Then write a formal email of 120–150 words to the coordinator suggesting one improvement to the booking system.`, speaking: [`Tell us about a practical skill you have learned.`,`Describe a place where people can learn from each other.`,`Compare learning a skill from a person with learning it online.`,`Some people believe everyone should learn basic repair skills. What is your opinion?`] },
  { reading: `When Hassan joined the youth radio project, he expected to spend most of his time speaking into a microphone. Instead, he learned how to plan interviews, check facts and edit recordings. The group broadcasts a weekly programme about issues chosen by local teenagers. Its latest series focused on public transport because many students travel long distances to school. Listeners sent in questions, and the team invited a transport officer to respond. Hassan says the project has helped him become more confident, but the biggest lesson was learning to listen carefully to different opinions. The station is now looking for a quiet room where volunteers can record without interruption.`, listening: `Host: Our guest today is Hassan from a youth radio project in Leeds. Hassan: I joined because I wanted to present a show, but I soon found that research and editing take just as much time. Our recent programme looked at school transport. We received more listener questions than expected. The hardest part is recording in a noisy shared room. We are asking the community centre if we can use a smaller office in the evenings.`, writing: `Your youth radio group is planning a new programme. Write an informal email of 40–50 words to a friend about a topic you would like to discuss. Then write a formal email of 120–150 words to the community centre requesting a quiet room for recording.`, speaking: [`What kind of radio or podcast programme do you enjoy?`,`Describe a time when you listened to someone with a different opinion.`,`Compare listening to a live broadcast with listening to a recording.`,`Should teenagers have more opportunities to speak about local issues? Explain your view.`] },
  { reading: `Clara first visited the riverside path as a runner. She later noticed that plastic packaging collected near a bend in the river after heavy rain. Rather than organise a single large clean-up, she invited residents to report litter through a simple online map. The reports showed that most rubbish gathered near two footbridges. Volunteers now check those areas every Sunday, while a local shop provides reusable gloves and bags. The council has agreed to install a sign explaining how visitors can help. Clara says the map has been more useful than she expected because it helps the group plan where to work. The next step is to compare the reports across different seasons.`, listening: `Interviewer: We are speaking with Clara, who coordinates a riverside project in York. Clara: We used to choose a clean-up location by guesswork. Then a resident suggested mapping the litter reports. The map showed that two footbridges needed attention most often. Volunteers now meet on Sunday mornings, but not every week in winter because the path can be unsafe. Our next goal is to see whether the new signs change visitor behaviour.`, writing: `You are a member of a riverside volunteer group. Write an informal email of 40–50 words to a friend describing a recent clean-up. Then write a formal email of 120–150 words to the council recommending a way to reduce litter near the river.`, speaking: [`Describe an outdoor place you like visiting.`,`Talk about a time you helped improve a shared space.`,`Compare cleaning a public place with protecting it through education.`,`Who should take greater responsibility for litter: local authorities or individuals?`] },
  { reading: `The museum in Bath used to close at five o'clock, when many people were still at work. A staff survey found that local residents wanted more time to visit after school and in the evening. The museum tested a late opening on one Thursday each month. At first, attendance was modest, so the team added short guided tours and a family activity table. Visitor numbers then increased, especially among people who had not visited before. The museum has kept the monthly evening programme but has not extended its opening hours every day. The director says the trial worked because staff changed the activities as well as the timetable.`, listening: `Reporter: The city museum has just completed a six-month evening-opening trial. Director Jon says the first two events were quiet. The team then added a short tour and activities for families. Attendance improved, and several visitors said they could only come after work. The museum will continue opening late once a month, but it cannot afford to staff the building every evening.`, writing: `You attended a museum evening event. Write an informal email of 40–50 words to a friend describing what you enjoyed. Then write a formal email of 120–150 words to the museum director suggesting an activity for future evening openings.`, speaking: [`What kind of museum or exhibition would you like to visit?`,`Describe an event that was more enjoyable than you expected.`,`Compare visiting a museum alone with visiting it in a group.`,`Should museums make more events free for local residents? Give reasons.`] },
  { reading: `Priya helped set up a food-sharing network after a community centre began receiving more donated vegetables than it could use. The team created a weekly collection point where residents can leave unopened food or take items they need. To make the service fair, volunteers record what arrives and ask people to take only what they can use. The network does not deliver meals, but it has helped several families reduce their shopping costs. Priya says clear information is essential because people sometimes assume that only certain residents are allowed to visit. The group is preparing a multilingual notice and hopes to recruit volunteers who can explain the service to newcomers.`, listening: `Presenter: Priya runs a food-sharing network in Oxford. Priya: The idea came from a community centre that had too many donated vegetables to store. We now have a collection table every Wednesday. People can take food without making an appointment. We do ask everyone to take a reasonable amount so that there is enough for late visitors. Our main task now is explaining clearly that anyone in the neighbourhood can use it.`, writing: `You recently used a local food-sharing point. Write an informal email of 40–50 words to a friend explaining how it works. Then write a formal email of 120–150 words to the community centre proposing how to make the service easier for new residents to understand.`, speaking: [`Tell us about a meal you enjoy sharing with others.`,`Describe a community service that could help local families.`,`Compare buying food in a shop with receiving food from a sharing scheme.`,`Should supermarkets be required to donate unsold food? Why?`] },
  { reading: `A school exchange between Cardiff and a partner school abroad began with letters between two classes. Teachers expected the main benefit to be language practice, but students also learned how school days can differ. Before the visit, each class prepared a short guide to its town. During the exchange, students worked in mixed groups on a project about local traditions. Some participants were nervous about speaking at first, so teachers paired them with patient partners. The schools plan to repeat the exchange online before arranging another visit. Their evaluation found that regular contact mattered more than the length of the trip.`, listening: `Teacher: We invited students from another school to join our project in Cardiff. The first online meeting was quiet because many students were unsure what to say. We then asked them to prepare questions in pairs. That made the conversation much easier. The in-person visit was useful, but the teachers agreed to keep monthly online meetings so the students can stay in touch between trips.`, writing: `Your school is organising an exchange visit. Write an informal email of 40–50 words to a friend describing one activity you hope to do. Then write a formal email of 120–150 words to the exchange coordinator suggesting how to help shy students take part.`, speaking: [`What subject did you enjoy most at school?`,`Describe a time you met someone from a different background.`,`Compare studying with a partner and studying independently.`,`Should schools spend more time teaching students about other cultures?`] },
  { reading: `Nora's sports club was created for adults who wanted to exercise but felt uncomfortable in competitive teams. The first sessions took place in a park and focused on walking and simple fitness games. Members later asked for indoor activities during bad weather. A nearby school agreed to lend its hall on Wednesday evenings, provided that the club finished before nine. The organisers keep the sessions low-cost by asking members to bring shared equipment. Nora says the most important change has been welcoming people at different fitness levels. The club has no plan to enter competitions; it wants to make regular activity part of people's week.`, listening: `Coach: The idea for our Brighton club came from a group of neighbours who wanted to be active without joining a competitive team. We began outdoors, but attendance dropped in winter. A local school now lends us its hall once a week. We cannot use it at weekends because other groups have booked it. New members say they like that nobody is expected to perform at a particular level.`, writing: `You tried a session at a volunteer sports club. Write an informal email of 40–50 words to a friend inviting them to join you. Then write a formal email of 120–150 words to the school asking to use its hall for one additional weekly session.`, speaking: [`What kind of exercise do you prefer?`,`Describe a time someone encouraged you to try an activity.`,`Compare exercising indoors with exercising outdoors.`,`Should local councils provide more free sports activities? Explain your answer.`] },
  { reading: `Ethan's science festival was designed to make research understandable to people who do not work in science. Instead of asking university staff to give long talks, the organisers invited them to demonstrate one idea using everyday objects. Visitors could ask questions at each table, and children received a small notebook to record what they discovered. The first festival was held in a school hall, which limited the number of visitors. This year the university has offered a larger space, but the team is keeping the same small-group format. Ethan believes this balance will allow more people to attend without making the demonstrations feel rushed.`, listening: `Presenter: The Cambridge science festival is moving to a larger venue this year. Organiser Ethan says the team considered adding a main stage, but visitors preferred being able to speak directly with researchers. They will therefore keep the hands-on tables and add more time slots. The university is lending a bigger building, while local volunteers will guide visitors between rooms.`, writing: `You visited a science festival. Write an informal email of 40–50 words to a friend describing the most interesting demonstration. Then write a formal email of 120–150 words to the organiser suggesting how the festival could welcome more visitors.`, speaking: [`What kind of science topic interests you?`,`Describe something new you learned recently.`,`Compare learning from a demonstration with learning from a book.`,`Should scientific research be explained to the general public? Why?`] },
  { reading: `A transport survey in Exeter asked residents how they travelled to work, school and local shops. The first questionnaire received few replies, so the team added paper copies at the library and a short version that could be completed on a phone. More people responded, including residents who rarely use social media. The results showed that many people would cycle for short trips if the roads felt safer. The council has not promised new cycle lanes yet, but it will use the survey to choose two streets for a safety review. The organisers plan to publish the results before the next budget meeting.`, listening: `Officer: Our travel survey in Exeter initially attracted mostly online responses. We wanted to hear from people who do not use social media, so we placed printed forms in the library and market. The responses showed that road safety was a bigger concern than journey length. We are now reviewing two streets near schools. The survey does not guarantee immediate construction work, but it gives us evidence for planning.`, writing: `You completed a local travel survey. Write an informal email of 40–50 words to a friend explaining one change you would like to see. Then write a formal email of 120–150 words to the council giving evidence-based suggestions for safer journeys.`, speaking: [`How do you usually travel around your town?`,`Describe a change that would make a journey easier for you.`,`Compare travelling by public transport with travelling by car.`,`Should city centres limit private cars? Give reasons for your view.`] },
  { reading: `The charity concert in Norwich was planned by a small group who had never organised a public event. They first expected to hire a professional sound company, but local musicians offered to bring equipment in exchange for a short rehearsal. A bookshop donated the room, which meant ticket prices could stay low. The concert raised less money than the organisers had hoped because several people bought tickets at the door rather than in advance. Even so, the shelter received enough to replace essential kitchen equipment. Ben says the group will publish its costs next time so supporters can see exactly how donations are used.`, listening: `Interviewer: Ben helped organise a charity concert in Norwich. Ben: Our biggest surprise was that local musicians offered to lend us their sound equipment. A bookshop also gave us the room, which helped us keep ticket prices affordable. We did not raise as much as planned because advance sales were slow. The shelter still received enough for new kitchen equipment. Next time, we want to share the budget before the event so people understand where the money goes.`, writing: `You attended a charity concert. Write an informal email of 40–50 words to a friend describing the evening. Then write a formal email of 120–150 words to the event organiser suggesting how to increase advance ticket sales.`, speaking: [`What kind of live music do you enjoy?`,`Describe an event you helped organise or would like to organise.`,`Compare attending a small concert with attending a large festival.`,`Is it better for charities to hold events or ask directly for donations? Explain.`] }
];
const q=(prompt,options,correct,kind)=>({prompt,options,correct,kind});
function balanceKeys(items, seed) {
  return items.map((item, index) => {
    const target = (seed + index) % item.options.length;
    const correctAnswer = item.options[item.correct];
    const distractors = item.options.filter((_, optionIndex) => optionIndex !== item.correct);
    const options = [...distractors]; options.splice(target, 0, correctAnswer);
    return { ...item, options, correct: target };
  });
}
